N={}
N[(1,0)]=[
("The idea","Think of a car: something has to start the engine, and something has to keep it running. The chapter's working definition: <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"motivation\">motivation</b> is the processes that <b>start</b> behaviour and <b>keep it going</b>. Definitions vary, but this one fits all the theories the chapter describes."),
("How the chapter sees students","Students are not simply \"motivated\" or \"unmotivated\". The chapter looks at their <b>beliefs, thoughts, goals and experiences</b> about themselves, and how these shape engagement and learning. These beliefs are thought to be conscious, partly emotional and often under the student's own control. <i>In plain words:</i> motivation lives in what students believe and aim for, so it can change."),
("One shared idea","All six theories share two ideas. <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"recdet\">Reciprocal determinism</b>: person, behaviour and environment shape each other (a student's beliefs steer what she does, what she does changes her classroom, the classroom shapes her beliefs). And <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"agency\">agency</b>: people organize, regulate and reflect on themselves; they are not pushed around like billiard balls."),
("The plan","Three parts: <b>six theories</b>, each with its core idea and the research on engagement, learning and achievement; work that <b>combines</b> theories; and directions for <b>future research</b>. A handbook chapter is a guided tour of a field, not one argument, and it tells you where evidence is strong, mixed or open."),
]
N[(2,0)]=[
("How to read it","Six frameworks drive most motivation research in education: <b>social cognitive theory</b>, <b>expectancy-value theory</b>, <b>interest</b>, <b>self-determination theory</b>, <b>achievement goal theory</b> and <b>attribution theory</b>. Each looks at the same student from a different side, so one sentence from a student usually fits one theory best, although the theories overlap."),
("What comes next","We take them in pairs (our grouping): <b>can I do it?</b> (self-efficacy, expectancy-value), <b>do I want to?</b> (interest, self-determination), and <b>what am I aiming for, and why did that happen?</b> (goals, attributions). Then how they fit together and what is unknown."),
]
N[(3,0)]=[
("The idea","Before a hard exam, one student thinks \"I can master this\", another \"this is beyond me\". <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"selfeff\">Self-efficacy</b> is your belief that you can carry out a task at a certain level: \"can I do this?\". It grows from four sources: <b>mastery experience</b> (your own success), <b>vicarious experience</b> (seeing others succeed), <b>social persuasion</b> (\"you can do it\") and <b>affective or physiological states</b> (calm or nerves)."),
("The evidence","Higher self-efficacy goes with better course and career choices, more effort and persistence even after failure, more self-regulatory strategies, more positive and fewer negative emotions, and higher achievement. <i>In plain words:</i> believing you can is linked to almost everything we want from a student."),
("What it is not","An <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"outexp\">outcome expectation</b> is believing an action leads to an outcome (\"if I study, I'll pass\"). <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"selfcon\">Self-concept</b> is a general judgment of your ability (\"I'm good at maths\"). You can believe studying leads to passing and still doubt you can do the studying: that doubt is low self-efficacy."),
("Recent refinements","<b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"calib\">Calibration</b>, the match between belief and performance: Chen (2003) found that calibration and self-efficacy each helped maths performance; Klassen (2007) found learning-disabled students less calibrated, more over-confident. <b>Collective efficacy</b>, a group's shared belief: teachers' collective efficacy predicts school achievement. <b>Sources:</b> students drawing on several sources at once had the highest science self-efficacy and achievement (Chen &amp; Usher, 2013)."),
("So what","The first answer to \"what starts and keeps a student going?\" is a belief: I can do this. But believing you can is not the same as wanting to. The next tab adds that question."),
]
N[(3,1)]=[
("The idea","Two beliefs are the closest predictors of students' choices and achievement: an <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"expect\">expectancy</b> (\"how well will I do?\") and <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"taskval\">task value</b> (\"how much is this worth to me?\"). Think of choosing a holiday: you want to expect it will work out, and you want it to be worth it."),
("The evidence","Expectancies most strongly predict <b>performance</b>, even controlling for earlier performance. Values most strongly predict <b>choices</b>, such as courses, sometimes years later. And they work together: each matters more when the other is high, and neither can make up for the other when it is low (Nagengast et al., 2011; Trautwein et al., 2012). <i>In plain words:</i> you need both \"I can\" and \"it's worth it\"."),
("Four kinds of value","<b>Utility value:</b> useful for other goals. <b>Attainment value:</b> doing well matters to who you are. <b>Intrinsic value:</b> interesting or fun. <b>Cost:</b> the downsides, such as effort or what you give up. Children tell competence apart from value early in primary school, and the kinds of value apart by about fifth grade. Expectancies differ from ability beliefs in theory, but not in the data."),
("What develops, and for whom","Expectancies and values predict persistence, performance and choice from first grade, more strongly with age, while the beliefs themselves decline, especially for language early on and maths in high school. Girls report higher expectancies and values for language; boys for maths, sports, science and engineering. <b>Cost</b> matters: effort and opportunity cost predicted intentions to leave STEM majors (Perez et al., 2014)."),
("So what","\"Can I do it?\" is half the story, and the smaller half when it comes to choices. A confident student who sees no worth in a subject will not choose it. The next step turns to wanting."),
]
N[(4,0)]=[
("The idea","A striking demo can spark anyone's attention for a lesson; a student who loves astronomy keeps returning to it for years. <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"situint\">Situational interest</b> comes from the context; <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"indint\">individual interest</b> is a lasting personal connection to a topic, with positive feelings and value."),
("The evidence","Activated interest supports attention, deeper processing and persistence while reading. <b>Rotgans and Schmidt (2011):</b> in a day of problem-based learning, situational interest predicted engagement, and through it achievement. <b>Harackiewicz and colleagues:</b> interest predicted task involvement and grades, and years later predicted course choice more strongly than achievement did."),
("Four phases","Situational interest is first <b>triggered</b> (a short spark, also called \"catch\") and then <b>maintained</b> (focused involvement and personal meaning, \"hold\"). Individual interest is <b>emerging</b> and then <b>well-developed</b>; Hidi and Renninger (2006) see knowledge as the catalyst between the two. Do not mix up emerging <i>situational</i> interest (another name for triggered) with emerging <i>individual</i> interest."),
("It is not always simple","Effects depend on competence, initial interest and prior achievement. <b>Durik and Harackiewicz (2007):</b> a triggering, flashy presentation helped students with low individual interest but undermined it for those with high interest; maintained interest helped only those who started high. Supports for moving from situational to individual interest: autonomy support, a friendly approachable instructor, chances to get involved, relevant material."),
("So what","A teacher cannot hand a student an individual interest, but can light sparks and keep them alive. Note the overlap with intrinsic value: the chapter later calls intrinsic motivation, task value and interest very similar constructs. Next: not how strong the wanting is, but whose it is."),
]
N[(4,1)]=[
("The idea","Two students study equally hard: one because she loves the subject, one because her parents will be angry otherwise. Self-determination theory asks about the <b>reason</b> for acting. <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"intrinsic\">Intrinsic motivation</b> means doing something for the activity itself; <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"extrinsic\">extrinsic motivation</b> means doing it for a separate outcome, such as praise or money. Extrinsic motivation varies in how far it has become your own: more <b>autonomous</b> or more <b>controlled</b>."),
("The forms in between","From most controlled to most autonomous: <b>external</b> (rewards, punishments), <b>introjected</b> (obligation, guilt, pride), <b>identified</b> (it is important for my goals) and <b>integrated</b> (it fits my core values), then intrinsic. Making a reason your own is <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"internal\">internalization</b>. It is fed by three innate needs: <b>autonomy</b>, <b>competence</b> and <b>relatedness</b>, important in both individualistic and collectivistic cultures."),
("The evidence","Intrinsic and other autonomous motivation go with creativity, engagement, deep learning strategies and achievement. More controlled forms predict, in many studies, poor strategies, anxiety, poorer coping, lower achievement and even dropout, though a few studies find extrinsic motivation helpful at times. Both intrinsic and extrinsic motivation tend to decline within and across school years."),
("What the classroom adds","Teachers support needs with meaningful choices, personal relevance, non-controlling language, room for opinions and negative feelings, feedback and structure. Controlling behaviour (commands, surveillance) can harm motivation. But effects are complex: choice may work best without pressure, in manageable amounts, for students who feel competent or interested or hold an upper-class Western outlook. Autonomy support and structure work together, and relatedness matters for engagement and achievement."),
("So what","The question shifts from \"how much motivation?\" to \"what kind?\". Students driven by rewards or guilt and students who own the reason may work equally hard today, but their learning, well-being and persistence tend to differ. Reading 2 is devoted to this theory."),
]
N[(5,0)]=[
("The idea","Two students prepare the same presentation: one wants to get better at presenting, the other wants to look smarter than the class. Achievement goal theory names two purposes: <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"mastery\">mastery</b> goals (<b>developing</b> competence) and <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"perfgoal\">performance</b> goals (<b>demonstrating</b> it). Crossed with <b>approach</b> versus <b>avoidance</b>, this gives four goals."),
("How the model grew","First two goals: mastery and performance. The <b>trichotomous model</b> (Elliot, 1999) split performance into approach (look competent) and avoidance (avoid looking incompetent). The <b>2 × 2 model</b> (Elliot &amp; McGregor, 2001) split mastery too: mastery-avoidance means avoiding losing skill or not reaching your potential. It is little studied and mostly goes with negative feelings, poor strategies and lower achievement, with some null or positive findings."),
("The evidence","Mastery-approach goals are clearly beneficial and performance-avoidance goals clearly harmful; performance-approach goals remain mixed and controversial. Links with achievement are debated and small. <b>Hulleman et al. (2010)</b>, a meta-analysis: mastery-approach .11, performance-approach .06, mastery-avoidance −.12, performance-avoidance −.13."),
("Why performance-approach is mixed","It depends on how it is measured: items about outperforming others correlated <b>positively</b> with achievement (.14); items about looking smart or showing ability correlated <b>negatively</b> (−.14). Combinations matter: <b>Luo et al. (2011)</b> found a profile of at least moderate mastery, high performance-approach and low performance-avoidance goals most beneficial. Goals are also shaped by the classroom's <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"goalstr\">goal structure</b>, and by motives and beliefs about intelligence."),
("So what","The same effort can serve very different purposes, and the purpose colours how a student reacts to difficulty: someone who wants to grow treats a mistake as information; someone afraid of looking incompetent hides it. What she then tells herself about the mistake is the next tab."),
]
N[(5,1)]=[
("The idea","After a failed test, one student thinks \"I didn't study enough\", another \"I'm just not smart\". Attribution theory assumes people want to understand their outcomes, especially unexpected or negative ones. The cause they pick (ability, effort, luck, task difficulty) is a <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"attrib\">causal attribution</b>. What matters is where it falls on three dimensions: <b>locus</b>, <b>stability</b> and <b>controllability</b>."),
("The three dimensions","<b>Locus</b>, internal (ability, effort) or external (luck, difficulty), relates to feelings such as pride and self-esteem. <b>Stability</b>, lasting (aptitude) or temporary (effort), relates most directly to expectancies for the future. <b>Controllability</b>, how much influence you have (effort yes, luck no), relates to hope, to shame and guilt, and to whether others help."),
("The evidence","Research generally fits the theory: attributions go with different emotions, expectancies and school functioning. Many interventions that train students to see successes as controllable and failures as unstable have beneficial effects. But one prediction does not consistently hold: effort attributions are not always better than ability attributions."),
("Connections to the other theories","Attributions link to goals and beliefs about intelligence: performance goals or a fixed view go with helpless attributions (failure caused by something internal, stable, uncontrollable), mastery goals or a malleable view with adaptive ones. <b>Perry et al. (2008):</b> moving into college, students who combined changeable internal causes (effort, strategy) with self-protective external ones (a hard test) did best. <b>Fulmer and Frijters (2011):</b> high topic interest shielded adolescents from excessive challenge."),
("So what","After every result a student writes a small story about the cause, and that story sets her expectation, her feeling and her next move. The six theories keep borrowing from each other: step 6 makes that overlap its subject."),
]
N[(6,0)]=[
("The idea","The six theories are the main roads, not the whole map. The authors single out <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"mindset\">mindsets</b>, or self-theories: beliefs about whether attributes such as intelligence are fixed or malleable. These beliefs may lie underneath several of the six theories."),
("The evidence","People with a more malleable view are more likely to adopt mastery goals, keep intrinsic interest, persist through challenge, perform better, make adaptive attributions and feel better overall. And beyond all six theories: <b>Bargh et al. (2001)</b> showed that people unknowingly primed with an achievement goal outperformed others and persisted longer on an anagram task. <i>In plain words:</i> some motivation works without our awareness."),
("The wider family","Other theories repeat the same themes: <b>personal control</b> theories; <b>control-value theory</b> (Pekrun, 2006), which explains achievement emotions from perceived control and value; <b>self-processes</b> (possible selves, self-concept, self-worth); <b>flow</b>, total absorption in an activity; <b>future time perspective</b>, the value of education for the future; and unconscious motivation, rarely studied in education but possibly powerful."),
("So what","So many theories keep finding the same things (competence, control, value, goals) that the next question is obvious: can they be combined?"),
]
N[(6,1)]=[
("The idea","A real student has self-efficacy, values, goals and explanations all at once. For over a decade, researchers have studied how forms of motivation from different theories <b>combine</b>. Three approaches: <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"varcent\">variable-centred</b> (what each kind adds, controlling for the others), <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"percent\">person-centred</b> (which profiles of motivation occur together), and <b>context-based</b> (how classrooms support several forms at once)."),
("Variable-centred","<b>Ciani et al. (2011)</b> joined self-determination and goal theory: need satisfaction went with mastery goals through autonomous motivation, and autonomy support slowed the decline of mastery goals over a semester. <b>Hulleman et al. (2008)</b> joined expectancy-value, interest and goals: intrinsic and utility value carried the effect of mastery-approach goals on later interest and grades. <i>In plain words:</i> one kind of motivation works through another."),
("Person-centred","Instead of one variable across everyone, look for combinations within people. <b>Conley (2012)</b> found seven profiles and needed two theories to predict feelings and achievement. <b>Shell and Husman (2008)</b> found groupings along three dimensions, such as a highly motivated, strategic learner versus an intrinsically motivated, mastery-focused one who used knowledge-building but not general strategies."),
("Context-based","Researchers also start from the classroom: <b>Turner et al. (2011)</b> derived best practices from several theories; Guthrie and Wigfield's concept-oriented reading program combines them in reading lessons; <b>Nolen (2007)</b> followed how several forms of reading and writing motivation developed in primary classrooms."),
("So what","The question has shifted from \"which theory is right?\" to \"how do the pieces work together in this student, in this classroom?\". The authors call the move toward how motivations combine within individuals a noteworthy advance. Reading 3 builds on this."),
]
N[(6,2)]=[
("The idea","A good overview is honest about its limits. The chapter closes with six directions for research, each pointing to a weak spot."),
("Directions 1 to 3","<b>(1) Too many names:</b> constructs about value (intrinsic motivation, task value, interest) and about competence (self-concept, self-efficacy, expectancies) are very similar; always check how a study defines and measures its terms. <b>(2) Integration with <span class=\"tm\" data-g=\"parsim\">parsimony</span>:</b> combining theories is promising, but very complex theories are unlikely to be used in practice. <b>(3) Mechanisms:</b> we know little about how situational interest becomes individual, what goal structures change, or how attribution retraining works."),
("Directions 4 to 6","<b>(4) Beyond social cognitive theories:</b> sociocultural and situated approaches look more at the person in context and may give more realistic guidelines. <b>(5) Culture:</b> move beyond listing group or country differences. <b>(6) Methods:</b> research leans heavily on <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"selfrep\">self-report</b>; students may misread items, and <b>Zhou and Winne (2012)</b> found that behavioural goal traces predicted test scores while self-reported goals did not. Observation, neuroimaging and implicit measures are suggested."),
("So what","Read every claim about motivation with two questions: how was it measured, and how was it defined? That habit separates a careful answer from a confident wrong one."),
]

